    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 131636\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n936 AM PDT Tue Oct 13 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* A cold front will move through the region today, bringing gusty \n  northwesterly winds and cooler temperatures to the region as well \n  as isolated showers and thunderstorms for the eastern half of our \n  forecast area. \n\n* A disturbance will bring another round of gusty north winds to \n  the region on Thursday and Friday.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...through the weekend.\n\nOvernight satellite imagery shows a low pressure system churning \nover the Great Basin with isolated convection across the \nsoutheastern Great Basin and northwestern Arizona. Isolated showers \nand thunderstorms will continue across the eastern half of our \nforecast area today, driven by the cold front associated with the \naforementioned low. Light-to-moderate rain, lightning, graupel, and \nerratic winds will be possible with storms today. In addition to \nshowers and thunderstorms, this cold front will also bring cooler \nthan normal temperatures to the region with the first freezes of the \nseason expected in the southwestern Great Basin early this morning. \nThe northern Owens Valley will have their shot at their first freeze \nof the season early tomorrow morning, as lighter winds and clear \nskies allow for stronger radiational cooling overnight. Gusty \nnorthwesterly winds will also accompany this front. The Inyo, \nEsmeralda, and central Nye counties will see peak wind gusts in the \n30 to 40 mph range with isolated wind gusts in excess of 40 mph \npossible in areas of higher terrain. Elsewhere, gusts will peak in \nthe 25 to 35 mph behind the cold front. \n\nOn Wednesday, the aforementioned low pressure system will move off \nto our east, with breezy north winds lingering down the Colorado \nRiver Valley and temperatures across the region beginning to \ngradually warm back up. A disturbance will sneak in behind the \nexiting low on Thursday, bringing another round of gusty north winds \nto the region Thursday afternoon through Friday. The strongest of\nthese winds are expected in the lower Colorado River Valley, where\nthe NBM boasts an 80+ percent chance of peak wind gusts in excess\nof 40 mph later this week. Winds will relax on Saturday and \nSunday, with temperatures returning to near normal over the \nweekend.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 18Z Forecast Package...Post \nfrontal northwesterly winds will continue with gusts to 30 knots \nthrough late morning along with light vicinity showers and SCT-BKN \nconditions between 7-10kft. Any shower activity should wane during \nafternoon with sky conditions improving. Winds will gradually ease \nthrough the late afternoon but remain northerly through evening and \novernight.\n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast \nCalifornia...For the 18Z Forecast Package...A cold front continues \nto push south this morning and is currently pushing into the central \nMojave Desert and Colorado River Valley. Behind the front, expect \ngusty north-northwest winds generally 20-35 knots. Precipitation \nchances (30-60%) should remain confined to southeastern NV and \nnorthwestern AZ. Near the shower activity, SCT-BKN conditions \nbetween 6-10kft. Farther south and west, anticipate FEW-SCT \nconditions at the same altitude. Winds weaken this evening, but \nretain a northerly direction in most locations.\n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Stessman\nAVIATION...Outler\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
